you may be wondering what the child in  this video did to deserve this young boy  I don't know that's aggressiveness but  14 year old Deshawn was simply walking  home from school please here explained  to a member of public that he was  stopped searched and pinned to the  ground because he matched the  description of a knife-wielding robber  DeSean was in his school uniform and the  only description that matched was the  color of desean's skin  they just snatched his mobile phone from  him they then proceeded to push him  against the railings and handcuff him  the way the incident was conducted is  now under investigation but Deshawn's  mum says it won't change the lasting  impact it's had on her son  Deshawn's education has been massively  affected by this um we've had to  organize transport to get him back and  forth from school he no longer goes out  with his friends anymore  um and of course there's a big distrust  the Met police say that stop and search  is a crucial tool in helping to reduce  crime but that it doesn't underestimate  the impact it can have on young people  recognizing the importance of using the  tool with dignity and respect  but that's not enough for Janet she's  petitioning for a change in law which  would see the police adopt a  child-centered approach to these  scenarios  the bill would support stop and search  as long as it's done the right way  Jaden is sympathetic to police he  understands that they have a job to do  but at just 15 years old he's been  stopped three times already to be honest  the situation I did have was a bit  humiliating because at the same time  there's people coming out of school  parents around kids adults and it was  just a bit embarrassing  a child's first encounter with police is  vitally important because it will shape  the way they view them for the rest of  their lives but a new report by  independent think tankers farm that when  it comes to black children the level of  trust in police is alarmingly low  73 percent of young people trusted the  police but only 36 percent of black  children trust the police  and whilst 87 percent of white children  would call the police if they believe  they're in danger only 56 percent of  black Caribbean children would call  police  how did you I put these findings to  deputy chief Constable Tyron Joyce he's  developing a plan of action to tackle  some of these issues  as a dad I feel a sense of Shame as a  police officer I feel a sense of Shame  because every member of the community  needs to feel protected the only thing  that I would say in this plan is there  is concrete activity to demonstrate that  we're being fair to demonstrate  proportionality to give that level of  reassurance but before building the  foundations of trust the police May  first have to confront and repair the  mistakes of the past Sherman Freeman  Powell Sky News
 

